#: source/language/builtins.md:33
msgid "**Tip**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:35
msgid "**Should I use `builtins` or `lib`?**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:37
msgid ""
"The built-ins are designed to be a stable interface that expressions can "
"depend on over time, so that, for instance, old Nixpkgs versions continue to "
"evaluate reproducibly."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:40
msgid ""
"On the flip side, this means they have accumulated a few quirks that Nix is "
"unable to change, but a library like Nixpkgs `lib` _can_ improve, replace or "
"deprecate those behaviors, because its sources are pinned where "
"reproducibility matters."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:44
msgid ""
"So while it is not wrong to use `builtins` directly, for instance in small "
"Nixpkgs-independent projects, you will have a better experience using a "
"library like `lib` as your primary source of functions, as it hides "
"problematic functions, fixes up others, and helps you improve your code by "
"means of future deprecations, which are still sufficiently rare."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:59
msgid "Abort Nix expression evaluation and print the error message _s_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:68
msgid "Return the sum of the numbers _e1_ and _e2_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:77
msgid ""
"Create a copy of the given string where a single [constant](../language/"
"string-context.md#string-context-constant) string context element is turned "
"into a [derivation deep](../language/string-context.md#string-context-"
"element-derivation-deep) string context element."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:83
msgid ""
"The store path that is the constant string context element should point to a "
"valid derivation, and end in `.drv`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:85
msgid ""
"The original string context element must not be empty or have multiple "
"elements, and it must not have any other type of element other than a "
"constant or derivation deep element. The latter is supported so this "
"function is idempotent."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:88
msgid ""
"This is the opposite of [`builtins.unsafeDiscardOutputDependency`](#builtins-"
"unsafeDiscardOutputDependency)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:97
msgid ""
"Evaluate _context_, which can be coerced to a string, and append it to any "
"error or stack traces displayed while evaluating _value_. Then return "
"_value_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:101
msgid ""
"This function is useful for providing helpful context in complex Nix "
"expressions when the evaluation of _value_ fails. The additional context is "
"applied when evaluating _value_ itself fails, not when attributes or "
"elements of _value_ are evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:106
msgid ""
"For example, the module system from nixpkgs uses this to show the relevant "
"information about the options that were evaluating when an error occurs."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:110
msgid ""
"```nix-repl\n"
"nix-repl> addErrorContext \"while evaluating foo\" (throw \"bar\")\n"
"error:\n"
"       … while evaluating foo\n"
"\n"
"       … while calling the 'throw' builtin\n"
"         at «string»:1:56:\n"
"            1| with builtins; addErrorContext \"while evaluating foo\" "
"(throw \"bar\")\n"
"             |                                                        ^\n"
"\n"
"       error: bar\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:130
msgid ""
"Return `true` if the function _pred_ returns `true` for all elements of "
"_list_, and `false` otherwise. Short-circuits and does not evaluate elements "
"that appear later in the list if `pred` evaluates to `false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:141
msgid ""
"Return `true` if the function _pred_ returns `true` for at least one element "
"of _list_, and `false` otherwise. Short-circuits and does not evaluate "
"elements that appear later in the list if `pred` evaluates to `true`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:152
msgid ""
"Return the names of the attributes in the set _set_ in an alphabetically "
"sorted list. For instance, `builtins.attrNames { y = 1; x = \"foo\"; }` "
"evaluates to `[ \"x\" \"y\" ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:156 source/language/builtins.md:168
msgid ""
"Has `O(n log n)` time complexity, where `n` is number of attributes in the "
"_set_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:165
msgid ""
"Return the values of the attributes in the set _set_ in the order "
"corresponding to the sorted attribute names."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:177
msgid ""
"Return the _base name_ of either a [path value](../language/types.md#type-"
"path) _x_ or a string _x_, depending on which type is passed, and according "
"to the following rules."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:179
msgid ""
"For a path value, the _base name_ is considered to be the part of the path "
"after the last directory separator, including any file extensions. This is "
"the simple case, as path values don't have trailing slashes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:182
msgid ""
"When the argument is a string, a more involved logic applies. If the string "
"ends with a `/`, only this one final slash is removed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:184
msgid ""
"After this, the _base name_ is returned as previously described, assuming `/"
"` as the directory separator. (Note that evaluation must be platform "
"independent.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:186
msgid ""
"This is somewhat similar to the [GNU `basename`](https://www.gnu.org/"
"software/coreutils/manual/html_node/basename-invocation.html) command, but "
"GNU `basename` strips any number of trailing slashes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:195
msgid "Return the bitwise AND of the integers _e1_ and _e2_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:204
msgid "Return the bitwise OR of the integers _e1_ and _e2_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:213
msgid "Return the bitwise XOR of the integers _e1_ and _e2_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:222
msgid ""
"In debug mode (enabled using `--debugger`), pause Nix expression evaluation "
"and enter the REPL. Otherwise, return the argument `v`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:232
msgid "Contains all the built-in functions and values."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:234
msgid ""
"Since built-in functions were added over time, [testing for attributes](./"
"operators.md#has-attribute) in `builtins` can be used for graceful fallback "
"on older Nix installations:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:248
msgid ""
"Collect each attribute named _attr_ from a list of attribute sets.  Attrsets "
"that don't contain the named attribute are ignored. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:252
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.catAttrs \"a\" [{a = 1;} {b = 0;} {a = 2;}]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:256
msgid "evaluates to `[1 2]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:258
msgid "Has `O(n)` time complexity, where `n` is the size of the _list_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:267
msgid ""
"Rounds and converts _number_ to the next higher NixInt value if possible, "
"i.e. `ceil *number* >= *number*` and `ceil *number* - *number* < 1`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:270
msgid ""
"An evaluation error is thrown, if there exists no such NixInt value `ceil "
"*number*`. Due to bugs in previous Nix versions an evaluation error might be "
"thrown, if the datatype of _number_ is a NixInt and if `*number* < "
"-9007199254740992` or `*number* > 9007199254740992`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:274
msgid ""
"If the datatype of _number_ is neither a NixInt (signed 64-bit integer) nor "
"a NixFloat (IEEE-754 double-precision floating-point number), an evaluation "
"error is thrown."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:284
msgid ""
"Compare two strings representing versions and return `-1` if version _s1_ is "
"older than version _s2_, `0` if they are the same, and `1` if _s1_ is newer "
"than _s2_. The version comparison algorithm is the same as the one used by "
"[`nix-env -u`](../command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:297
msgid "Concatenate a list of lists into a single list."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:306
msgid ""
"This function is equivalent to `builtins.concatLists (map f list)` but is "
"more efficient."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:316
msgid ""
"Concatenate a list of strings with a separator between each element, e.g. "
"`concatStringsSep \"/\" [\"usr\" \"local\" \"bin\"] == \"usr/local/bin\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:327
msgid ""
"Return the specified representation of a hash string, based on the "
"attributes presented in _args_:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:329
msgid "`hash`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:331
msgid "The hash to be converted. The hash format is detected automatically."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:334
msgid "`hashAlgo`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:336
msgid "The algorithm used to create the hash. Must be one of"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:337
msgid "`\"md5\"`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:338
msgid "`\"sha1\"`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:339
msgid "`\"sha256\"`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:340
msgid "`\"sha512\"`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:342
msgid ""
"The attribute may be omitted when `hash` is an [SRI hash](https://www.w3.org/"
"TR/SRI/#the-integrity-attribute) or when the hash is prefixed with the hash "
"algorithm name followed by a colon. That `<hashAlgo>:<hashBody>` syntax is "
"supported for backwards compatibility with existing tooling."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:345
msgid "`toHashFormat`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:347
msgid "The format of the resulting hash. Must be one of"
