msgid ""
"Return the substring of _s_ from byte position _start_ (zero-based) up to "
"but not including _start + len_. If _start_ is greater than the length of "
"the string, an empty string is returned. If _start + len_ lies beyond the "
"end of the string or _len_ is `-1`, only the substring up to the end of the "
"string is returned. _start_ must be non-negative. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2775
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.substring 0 3 \"nixos\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2779
msgid "evaluates to `\"nix\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2788
msgid ""
"Return the list without its first item; abort evaluation if the argument "
"isn’t a list or is an empty list."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2793
msgid ""
"This function should generally be avoided since it's inefficient: unlike "
"Haskell's `tail`, it takes O(n) time, so recursing over a list by repeatedly "
"calling `tail` takes O(n^2) time."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2804
msgid ""
"Throw an error message _s_. This usually aborts Nix expression evaluation, "
"but in `nix-env -qa` and other commands that try to evaluate a set of "
"derivations to get information about those derivations, a derivation that "
"throws an error is silently skipped (which is not the case for `abort`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2817
msgid ""
"Store the string _s_ in a file in the Nix store and return its path.  The "
"file has suffix _name_. This file can be used as an input to derivations. "
"One application is to write builders “inline”. For instance, the following "
"Nix expression combines the Nix expression for GNU Hello and its build "
"script into one file:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2823
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ stdenv, fetchurl, perl }:\n"
"\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation {\n"
"  name = \"hello-2.1.1\";\n"
"\n"
"  builder = builtins.toFile \"builder.sh\" \"\n"
"    source $stdenv/setup\n"
"\n"
"    PATH=$perl/bin:$PATH\n"
"\n"
"    tar xvfz $src\n"
"    cd hello-*\n"
"    ./configure --prefix=$out\n"
"    make\n"
"    make install\n"
"  \";\n"
"\n"
"  src = fetchurl {\n"
"    url = \"http://ftp.nluug.nl/pub/gnu/hello/hello-2.1.1.tar.gz\";\n"
"    sha256 = \"1md7jsfd8pa45z73bz1kszpp01yw6x5ljkjk2hx7wl800any6465\";\n"
"  };\n"
"  inherit perl;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2849
msgid "It is even possible for one file to refer to another, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2851
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builder = let\n"
"  configFile = builtins.toFile \"foo.conf\" \"\n"
"    # This is some dummy configuration file.\n"
"    ...\n"
"  \";\n"
"in builtins.toFile \"builder.sh\" \"\n"
"  source $stdenv/setup\n"
"  ...\n"
"  cp ${configFile} $out/etc/foo.conf\n"
"\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2864
msgid ""
"Note that `${configFile}` is a [string interpolation](../language/"
"types.md#type-string), so the result of the expression `configFile` (i.e., a "
"path like `/nix/store/m7p7jfny445k...-foo.conf`) will be spliced into the "
"resulting string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2870
msgid ""
"It is however _not_ allowed to have files mutually referring to each other, "
"like so:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2873
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  foo = builtins.toFile \"foo\" \"...${bar}...\";\n"
"  bar = builtins.toFile \"bar\" \"...${foo}...\";\n"
"in foo\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2880
msgid ""
"This is not allowed because it would cause a cyclic dependency in the "
"computation of the cryptographic hashes for `foo` and `bar`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2883
msgid ""
"It is also not possible to reference the result of a derivation. If you are "
"using Nixpkgs, the `writeTextFile` function is able to do that."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2894
msgid ""
"Return a string containing a JSON representation of _e_. Strings, integers, "
"floats, booleans, nulls and lists are mapped to their JSON equivalents. Sets "
"(except derivations) are represented as objects. Derivations are translated "
"to a JSON string containing the derivation’s output path. Paths are copied "
"to the store and represented as a JSON string of the resulting store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2908
msgid ""
"**DEPRECATED.** Use `/. + \"/path\"` to convert a string into an absolute "
"path. For relative paths, use `./. + \"/path\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2918
msgid "Convert the expression _e_ to a string. _e_ can be:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2920
msgid "A string (in which case the string is returned unmodified)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2922
msgid "A path (e.g., `toString /foo/bar` yields `\"/foo/bar\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2924
msgid "A set containing `{ __toString = self: ...; }` or `{ outPath = ...; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2926
msgid "An integer."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2928
msgid ""
"A list, in which case the string representations of its elements are joined "
"with spaces."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2931
msgid "A Boolean (`false` yields `\"\"`, `true` yields `\"1\"`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2933
msgid "`null`, which yields the empty string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2942
msgid ""
"Return a string containing an XML representation of _e_. The main "
"application for `toXML` is to communicate information with the builder in a "
"more structured format than plain environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2947
msgid "Here is an example where this is the case:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2949
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ stdenv, fetchurl, libxslt, jira, uberwiki }:\n"
"\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation (rec {\n"
"  name = \"web-server\";\n"
"\n"
"  buildInputs = [ libxslt ];\n"
"\n"
"  builder = builtins.toFile \"builder.sh\" \"\n"
"    source $stdenv/setup\n"
"    mkdir $out\n"
"    echo \"$servlets\" | xsltproc ${stylesheet} - > $out/server-conf.xml ①\n"
"  \";\n"
"\n"
"  stylesheet = builtins.toFile \"stylesheet.xsl\" ②\n"
"   \"<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>\n"
"    <xsl:stylesheet xmlns:xsl='http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform' "
"version='1.0'>\n"
"      <xsl:template match='/'>\n"
"        <Configure>\n"
"          <xsl:for-each select='/expr/list/attrs'>\n"
"            <Call name='addWebApplication'>\n"
"              <Arg><xsl:value-of select=\\\"attr[@name = 'path']/string/"
"@value\\\" /></Arg>\n"
"              <Arg><xsl:value-of select=\\\"attr[@name = 'war']/path/@value\\"
"\" /></Arg>\n"
"            </Call>\n"
"          </xsl:for-each>\n"
"        </Configure>\n"
"      </xsl:template>\n"
"    </xsl:stylesheet>\n"
"  \";\n"
"\n"
"  servlets = builtins.toXML [ ③\n"
"    { path = \"/bugtracker\"; war = jira + \"/lib/atlassian-jira.war\"; }\n"
"    { path = \"/wiki\"; war = uberwiki + \"/uberwiki.war\"; }\n"
"  ];\n"
"})\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2986
msgid ""
"The builder is supposed to generate the configuration file for a [Jetty "
"servlet container](http://jetty.mortbay.org/). A servlet container contains "
"a number of servlets (`*.war` files) each exported under a specific URI "
"prefix. So the servlet configuration is a list of sets containing the `path` "
"and `war` of the servlet (①). This kind of information is difficult to "
"communicate with the normal method of passing information through an "
"environment variable, which just concatenates everything together into a "
"string (which might just work in this case, but wouldn’t work if fields are "
"optional or contain lists themselves). Instead the Nix expression is "
"converted to an XML representation with `toXML`, which is unambiguous and "
"can easily be processed with the appropriate tools. For instance, in the "
"example an XSLT stylesheet (at point ②) is applied to it (at point ①) to "
"generate the XML configuration file for the Jetty server. The XML "
"representation produced at point ③ by `toXML` is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3004
msgid "'1.0'"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3004
msgid "'utf-8'"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3008 source/language/builtins.md:3016
msgid "\"path\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3009
msgid "\"/bugtracker\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3011 source/language/builtins.md:3019
msgid "\"war\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3012
msgid "\"/nix/store/d1jh9pasa7k2...-jira/lib/atlassian-jira.war\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3017
msgid "\"/wiki\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3020
msgid "\"/nix/store/y6423b1yi4sx...-uberwiki/uberwiki.war\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3027
msgid ""
"Note that we used the `toFile` built-in to write the builder and the "
"stylesheet “inline” in the Nix expression. The path of the stylesheet is "
"spliced into the builder using the syntax `xsltproc ${stylesheet}`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3039
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_ and print its abstract syntax representation on standard "
"error. Then return _e2_. This function is useful for debugging."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3043
msgid ""
"If the [`debugger-on-trace`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-debugger-on-"
"trace) option is set to `true` and the `--debugger` flag is given, the "
"interactive debugger is started when `trace` is called (like [`break`](../"
"language/builtins.md#builtins-break))."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3056
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_ and print its abstract syntax representation on standard error "
"if `--trace-verbose` is enabled. Then return _e2_. This function is useful "
"for debugging."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3074
msgid "The name `true` is not special, and can be shadowed:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3088
msgid ""
"Try to shallowly evaluate _e_. Return a set containing the attributes "
"`success` (`true` if _e_ evaluated successfully, `false` if an error was "
"thrown) and `value`, equalling _e_ if successful and `false` otherwise. "
"`tryEval` only prevents errors created by `throw` or `assert` from being "
"thrown. Errors `tryEval` doesn't catch are, for example, those created by "
"`abort` and type errors generated by builtins. Also note that this doesn't "
"evaluate _e_ deeply, so `let e = { x = throw \"\"; }; in (builtins.tryEval "
"e).success` is `true`. Using `builtins.deepSeq` one can get the expected "
"result: `let e = { x = throw \"\"; }; in (builtins.tryEval (builtins.deepSeq "
"e e)).success` is `false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3102
msgid ""
"`tryEval` intentionally does not return the error message, because that "
"risks bringing non-determinism into the evaluation result, and it would "
"become very difficult to improve error reporting without breaking existing "
"expressions. Instead, use [`builtins.addErrorContext`](../language/"
"builtins.md#builtins-addErrorContext) to add context to the error message, "
"and use a Nix unit testing tool for testing."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3112
msgid ""
"Return a string representing the type of the value _e_, namely `\"int\"`, "
"`\"bool\"`, `\"string\"`, `\"path\"`, `\"null\"`, `\"set\"`, `\"list\"`, "
"`\"lambda\"` or `\"float\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3123
msgid ""
"Create a copy of the given string where every [derivation deep](../language/"
"string-context.md#string-context-element-derivation-deep) string context "
"element is turned into a [constant](../language/string-context.md#string-"
"context-constant) string context element."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3129
msgid ""
"This is the opposite of [`builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies`](#builtins-"
"addDrvOutputDependencies)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3131
msgid ""
"This is unsafe because it allows us to \"forget\" store objects we would "
"have otherwise referred to with the string context, whereas Nix normally "
"tracks all dependencies consistently. Safe operations \"grow\" but never "
"\"shrink\" string contexts. [`builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies`](#builtins-"
"addDrvOutputDependencies) in contrast is safe because \"derivation deep\" "
"string context element always refers to the underlying derivation (among "
"many more things). Replacing a constant string context element with a "
"\"derivation deep\" element is a safe operation that just enlargens the "
"string context without forgetting anything."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3146
msgid ""
"Discard the [string context](../language/string-context.md) from a value "
"that can be coerced to a string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3155
msgid ""
"`unsafeGetAttrPos` returns the position of the attribute named _s_ from "
"_set_. This is used by Nixpkgs to provide location information in error "
"messages."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3166
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_, which must be a string, and print it on standard error as a "
"warning. Then return _e2_. This function is useful for non-critical "
"situations where attention is advisable."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3170
msgid ""
"If the [`debugger-on-trace`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-debugger-on-"
"trace) or [`debugger-on-warn`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-debugger-on-"
"warn) option is set to `true` and the `--debugger` flag is given, the "
"interactive debugger will be started when `warn` is called (like [`break`]"
"(../language/builtins.md#builtins-break))."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3177
msgid ""
"If the [`abort-on-warn`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-abort-on-warn) "
"option is set, the evaluation is aborted after the warning is printed. This "
"is useful to reveal the stack trace of the warning, when the context is non-"
"interactive and a debugger can not be launched."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3189
msgid ""
"Transpose a list of attribute sets into an attribute set of lists, then "
"apply `mapAttrs`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3192
msgid ""
"`f` receives two arguments: the attribute name and a non-empty list of all "
"values encountered for that attribute name."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3195
msgid ""
"The result is an attribute set where the attribute names are the union of "
"the attribute names in each element of `list`. The attribute values are the "
"return values of `f`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3199
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.zipAttrsWith\n"
"  (name: values: { inherit name values; })\n"
"  [ { a = \"x\"; } { a = \"y\"; b = \"z\"; } ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3207
msgid ""
"```\n"
"{\n"
"  a = { name = \"a\"; values = [ \"x\" \"y\" ]; };\n"
"  b = { name = \"b\"; values = [ \"z\" ]; };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3214
msgid ""
"Has `O(n log n)` time complexity, where `n` is the number of attributes "
"across all sets."
msgstr ""
